   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil service law, in relation to eliminating the \"wartime\nservice\" requirement",
    "basePrintNo": "S7378",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7378-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §55-c, Civ Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7378\n\nSPONSOR: SCARCELLA-SPANTON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil service law, in relation to eliminating the\n\"wartime service\" requirement\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nEnlarge the application pool of disabled Veterans who qualify for\nSection 55-c State employment by eliminating the \"wartime service\"\nrequirement that prohibits a large portion of interested disabled Veter-\nans from seeking State employment under the Section 55-c program.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nAmend Section 55-c of the New York State Civil Service Law by removing\nthe \"wartime service\" requirement, opening the New York State employment\nopportunities to many more disabled Veterans.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nNew York State Civil Service Law, Article 4, Section 55-c requires that\nCivil Services set aside 500 State employment slots for disabled Veter-\nans who served during a \"time of war.\" Section 55-c allows Veterans to\nenter a noncompetitive employment list without taking the Civil Service\nExam if otherwise qualified for the position.\n\nApplicants must submit a copy of their DD-214, or other discharge docu-\nment, and proof that they that they have at least a 10% disability\nrating from the U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs to be eligible under\n55-c.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n \nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nOn Veterans Day 2021, Governor Hochul signed into law a much-welcomed\namendment to Section 85 of New York State's Civil Service Law, removing\nthe \"wartime era\" requirement for Veterans to qualify for tax\nexemptions, merit scholarships, and additional credits towards Civil\nService employment and promotions.\n\nInexplicitly, Section 55-c still refers to Section 85 to define \"wartime\nservice\" even though Section 85 no longer provides a definition of\n\"wartime service.\" Last year's amendment to Section 85 was supposed to\nremove the wartime service requirement from all the Civil Service\nprograms for Veterans. Such belief is consistent with the Governor's\nobjective of bringing more Veterans into the New York State workforce.\nIf the Legislature had intended to keep the wartime requirement for\nSection 55-c, the Legislature could have so stated by leaving the defi-\nnition of \"wartime service\" in Section 85 or moving the definition of\n\"wartime service\" into 55-c. Instead, the Section 55-c program refers to\na definition of \"wartime service\" that no longer exists.\n\n\nThe term \"in time of war\" is a term of art that, unless otherwise\ndefined, refers to wars declared by Congress. Unless and until this term\nis specifically redefined, only those who served in World War II or\nbefore are eligible for the Section 55-c program.\n\nMoreover, Section 55-c is not functioning as intended because only 20%\nof the positions meant to be filled by disabled Veterans are currently\noccupied. This is an indication that the law needs to be amended, and\nthis can be easily accomplished by removing the wartime service require-\nment. This will allow 55-c to operate the way that the Governor and\nLegislature originally intended.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone. Section 55-c already sets aside 500 positions for eligible disa-\nbled Veterans, and currently only about 20% of those positions are occu-\npied. No new job opportunities would be created by the State, the\nproposed amendment opens vacant positions to being filled by a wider\npool of qualified candidates.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
